<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Typographic tokens for a right-to-left interface — SPEC.md §7, §9.

    One token changes, and only under this qualifier: the tightening the
    titles are set with.

    Bricolage Grotesque holds no Arabic letter — its character map stops at
    527 glyphs, none of them in the Arabic block — so an Arabic title is drawn
    by the system's fallback face. The hundredth of an em the titles are drawn
    together by was decided for Bricolage's own letterforms; on letters it
    does not draw, it is an instruction about a drawing that is not there.

    It also breaks the title. Arabic is cursive: its letters join, and their
    shapes depend on their neighbours. Asking for letter spacing changes how
    that run is shaped, and the two ways Android measures a line stop
    agreeing. `Toolbar` builds its title as a single-line, end-ellipsized
    `TextView`, and sizes it to exactly the width the measuring path returns —
    197 px for "settings" on a 1080 px screen, kerning or no kerning, which is
    the measuring path telling us it never applied the kerning at all. The
    laying-out path does apply it, comes out some eight pixels wider than the
    box it was given, and the ellipsis fires: the settings screen read
    "الإعدادا…" and the journey screen "الرح…", each with most of the bar
    empty beside it.

    Latin, Greek, Chinese and Japanese titles are untouched by the whole
    thing — measured whole and drawn whole, kerning included — because their
    characters map one to one onto glyphs and the two paths agree. Which is
    why the token is withdrawn here rather than everywhere: the tightening is
    the project's typography and it stays exactly as it was on every script
    that can carry it.

    Zero rather than a smaller negative: there is no kerning that is safe on a
    cursive script, only one that has not yet been caught out by a longer
    title.
-->
<resources>

    <item name="title_letter_spacing" format="float" type="dimen">0.0</item>

</resources>
